{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"censorian-goldleaf-cicada","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Impact, stated first\n\nEvery live Reconcile Bridge pass re-attempts a set of links that **cannot ever succeed** — the source ticket is closed, the relationship is redundant, or the link would create a cycle. The attempts fail, the pass moves on, and the next pass tries the identical set again. The bridge therefore **never reaches a quiet steady state**, and its log carries a permanent floor of errors that must be mentally filtered before a real fault is visible.\n\nFound by an automated ops error sweep of GitHub Actions over 2026-08-05 -> 2026-08-12T03:30Z. Present in **every** live pass sampled from 2026-08-06 through 2026-08-11. No open ticket covered it.\n\n## The three failure shapes, verbatim\n\n    rebar.link failed ... Error: cannot create blocks link — source ticket `a880-...` is closed\n    ERROR: redundant link — ... ancestor-descendant relationship\n    Adding 42eb-... -> 0303-... (blocks) would create a cycle at epic level\n\n## Why this is a real defect and not cosmetic\n\nThree distinct harms, in order of how much they cost:\n\n1. **It masks real faults.** This bug shares a workflow with the ACLI failures filed as [rebar:9a71-ba9b-89d0-4cb8], and the mid-pass `command cancelled` abort in that ticket appears *after a long batch of these very warnings*. A log with a permanent error floor is exactly the condition under which a new, genuine error goes unnoticed — and during this sweep the churn had to be manually separated from the real signal before the ACLI abort could be seen at all.\n2. **It burns a Jira round-trip per impossible link, every pass.** The passes run continuously (the workflow re-dispatches itself), so this is a standing cost paid indefinitely for work whose outcome is known in advance.\n3. **It defeats convergence as an observable property.** \"Did this pass change anything?\" is the cheapest health question you can ask a reconciler. Right now the answer is always yes, so the question is useless — and the live Jira DC suite already asserts convergence as an invariant (`tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_reconcile_pass.py`).\n\n## The shape of the fix (not prescribing the implementation)\n\nAll three shapes are **decidable before the write is attempted** — closedness of the source, ancestry between the endpoints, and cycle formation are all answerable from state the reconciler already has. So the question is not \"how do we retry more gracefully\" but \"why is an undecidable-looking write being attempted at all when the outcome is computable\".\n\nA caution worth recording: simply suppressing the log lines would satisfy the noise complaint while leaving harms 2 and 3 fully intact, and would make the churn *less* visible rather than absent. That is not a fix.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A link whose failure is decidable in advance (closed source, redundant ancestor-descendant, cycle-forming) is not attempted against Jira on repeat passes.\n- [ ] A live reconcile pass over an unchanged store reports convergence — it writes nothing and logs no link errors.\n- [ ] The decision to skip is recorded somewhere durable enough that a genuinely new impossible link is still visible to an operator, rather than being silently swallowed.\n- [ ] Evidence that the fix is not merely log suppression: the count of Jira link write attempts per steady-state pass drops to zero for these three shapes.\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled by an automated ops error sweep. No code was changed. No file_impact is recorded — this sweep did not trace the link-apply path to specific modules, and guessing would be worse than leaving it undeclared.","id":"b8b1-6462-b4f1-4a1d","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Reconciler re-attempts the same structurally-impossible Jira->rebar links on EVERY pass, so the bridge never converges and its log is permanently noisy"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786505787239433001,"uuid":"210f8627-d6ce-41c4-9df9-241d0d67e27d"}